# Memory guardrails (Shipwright)

## Fail-closed enforcement (A1)

Three layers — do not conflate them:

| Layer | What | Greenfield (zero memories) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Static** | `.cursor/rules/sw-guardrails.mdc` (`alwaysApply`) | Always on — baseline plugin guardrails without Recallium |
| **Dynamic** | Rule-class memories from the provider, filtered by allowlist | Empty is **normal** for a new repo / new `memory.project` |
| **Hook** | `beforeSubmitPrompt` fail-closed check | Blocks only when the provider is **unreachable** (cannot confirm state) |

Repos **without** `workflow.config.json` are not gated (global plugin install must not block unrelated workspaces).

When opted in (`workflow.config.json` present) and `memory.guardrails.enforceBeforeSubmit` is true (default):

- Provider unreachable → **block** (A1 / R44 — cannot confirm guardrail state).
- Provider reachable, zero rule-class memories → **pass** (confirmed empty; not a failure).
- `memory.guardrails.requireRuleClass: true` → **block** until at least one allowlisted rule-class memory exists (strict / mature repos only).

Set `memory.guardrails.enforceBeforeSubmit: false` to disable the hook entirely (plugin development, temporary bypass).

`sessionStart` injection is best-effort (fail-open).

## Write binding hard-cut (unbound refuse + assert)

Mutating memory paths (`/sw-memory-sync` store, adapter `store`, and other R9-enumerated writers) MUST
call `scripts/memory_preflight.py` write assert (`assert_write_binding` / `assert-sync-store` /
`resolve-provider --for-write`) **immediately before** provider dispatch. Unbound writes refuse with a
typed cause and audit event — never ambient-default to Recallium or omit `project_name` into a
machine-local MCP project.

| Binding | Condition | Write project |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Config | `memory.provider` **and** non-empty `memory.project` | configured project |
| Marker | `.cursor/sw-memory.provider` literal `in-repo` | workspace basename |
| Unbound | neither | **refuse** (`writeAuthorized=false`) |

Remote/external markers without `memory.project` refuse. Unbound **reads**/preflight MAY resolve for
display (`source=unbound`, `displayGuidance=in-repo`) without authorizing writes. Hard cut on ship: no
soft-warn write window; doctor/`/sw-init` tell unbound operators how to bind before sync.

## Catalog resolve + validator (PRD 071 R4)

Before hook rule-fetch or adapter load, Shipwright validates `memory.provider` through the catalog +
`scripts/memory_provider_register.py` (charset, catalog row, adapter integrity, rules script). **Catalog
membership alone does not grant hook trust** — `capability_trust.py` execution gates remain authoritative.

| State | Hook `beforeSubmitPrompt` | Rule-fetch |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unknown / invalid id | Fail closed — provider not resolved | No script; no cross-provider fallback |
| Registered but unreachable | **Block** (cannot confirm guardrail state) | No rules injected |
| Reachable, zero rule-class memories | **Pass** (confirmed empty) | Empty rules list — non-blocking |
| Reachable with rules | Pass after allowlist filter | Provider's registered rules script only |

Authors register providers in `.sw/memory-provider-catalog.json`; operators only select registered ids in
config or `.cursor/sw-memory.provider`.

## Planning store memory backend (PRD 034 R11/R25)

The `planning.store` memory backend routes exclusively through the provider-agnostic memory adapter and
`scripts/memory-redact.py` on both read and write. It never calls provider MCP tools directly, bans
`discussion`/`progress` classes for planning bodies, and refuses raw transcript content.

## Redaction chokepoint (R41 / PRD 082 R32)

Before any memory persist or re-injection, run the shared redaction filter on:

- transcript deltas (`/sw-memory-sync`)
- deploy-log edges (deferred workstream)
- Sentry payload edges (deferred workstream)

### Destination-aware contract (fail-closed)

`scripts/memory_redact.py` requires an explicit `--destination` tier (`local` | `committed` | `external` |
`cross-project` | `logs`) — **no default**. Callers that omit `--destination` must fail closed rather than
infer a tier. Mandatory removal destinations (`external`, `cross-project`, `logs`) raise when residual
secret patterns survive substitution. Derived learning-store admissions and exogenous routing payloads
use the same filter — no bypass for analytics or graph provenance writes.

Planning and memory emission points bind to destinations via `scripts/planning_visibility.py`
(`resolve_emission_destination` / `EMISSION_POINT_DESTINATIONS`). Unregistered emission points resolve to
`external` (strictest tier). Redaction at each emission point uses the bound destination — not ambient
context.

Named patterns: AWS `AKIA…`, `ghp_…`/PATs, JWTs, `Bearer` headers, PEM private keys, email addresses.

## Rule-class promotion (R42)

`category: rule` requires human confirmation + provenance metadata. Auto-distilled candidates cannot
promote without `/sw-memory-audit` approval. Active rules must appear in `.cursor/sw-memory-rule-allowlist.json`.

**D3 — human-gated promotion is unchanged.** Provider-aware storage does not add an automatic promote
path. `/sw-memory-audit` remains the only approval command; allowlist membership still gates injection.

**D4 — planningIssues are planning-store ids.** Rule-related planning artifacts identify units by
planning-store ids (for example `277-prd-provider-aware-rule-storage` / `tasks-277-provider-aware-rule-storage`),
not host issue numbers as the durable identity.

## Trust boundary (R43)

Memory provider holds project-scoped operational data. Least-privilege API credentials via environment only.
Compromised provider → rotate credentials, audit allowlist, re-export neutral JSONL.

## Source of truth — decision class (PRD 015)

`memory.sourceOfTruth` (`repo` | `memory` | `auto`, default `auto`) selects which side is authoritative for
**frozen decision records** only. Other memory categories remain distillation-only.

| Effective SoT | Authoritative | Non-authoritative |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `repo` (default `auto`+in-repo) | git decision record | provider pointer via `relatedFiles` |
| `memory` | provider `decision` record (redacted) | committed git snapshot pointer |

Resolve via `python3 scripts/memory-sot.py resolve --class decision --json`. Switching modes requires an
explicit operator change plus `/sw-memory-audit` + `memory-sot-audit.py legacy-reconcile-plan`. Redaction
(R41) and rule-class gates (R42) apply identically in both modes.
